Subject: HDMA and the vblank writes start each frame the way the console does

Two fixes in the frame renderer, found against Snes9x on Super Mario All-Stars (sky gradients):
- Writes the game made during vblank are applied before HDMA's V=0 init, not after its first
  transfer, so they no longer overwrite line 0.
- Line 0 is not shown but its H-blank still steps HDMA, so every HDMA band edge was one line late.
diff --git a/runner/src/beam_frame_driver.c b/runner/src/beam_frame_driver.c
index 66ea098..38b0c91 100644
--- a/runner/src/beam_frame_driver.c
+++ b/runner/src/beam_frame_driver.c
@@ -254,10 +254,15 @@ void snes_beam_frame_driver_draw_ppu_frame_observed(
    * the beam must not also step it from inside guest register writes. */
   snes_set_hdma_beam_enabled(g_snes, false);
   ppu_rasterRenderBegin(g_ppu);
+  /* Writes made during vblank (V=225..261) precede V=0's HDMA init on hardware;
+   * applied later, they would overwrite HDMA's first-line values on line 0. */
+  ppu_rasterApplyLine(g_ppu, 261);
   dma_initHdma(g_snes->dma);
   /* Hardware's V=0 init also performs each channel's first transfer before
    * any visible pixel. */
   dma_primeHdmaFirstLine(g_snes->dma);
+  /* Line 0 is never shown, but its H-blank still steps HDMA (Snes9x agrees). */
+  dma_doHdma(g_snes->dma);
   for (line = 1; line <= 224; line++) {
     uint8_t hdmaen;
